;; `bjo` applied to something that is not a call.
;;
;; The form splits an application into operands evaluated here and a call made
;; over there. There is nothing to split in a bare name, and letting one through
;; would quietly mean "spawn a fiber that evaluates a variable", which is not
;; something anyone wants to have written.
;;
;; The rejection also keeps the distinction between the special form and
;; `spawn-thunk` visible. A special form is not a value, so `(map bjo thunks)`
;; cannot work; `spawn-thunk` is the function to reach for, and the error says
;; so rather than leaving the reader to find it.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: Invalid bjo syntax
;; EXPECT-ERROR: use spawn-thunk

(import (std prelude))

(: work (-> int int))
(defbjo (work x) (* x 2))

(defbjo (main args)
  (def p (bjo work))
  0)
